.TH PHOTOC 1 "October 6, 2026" "photoc" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
photoc \- Command-line tools for photographers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B photoc
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B photoc
.I command
.B \-\-help
.br
.B photoc \-\-help
.br
.B photoc \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B photoc
is a command-line toolkit for photographers working with local files and
repeatable shell workflows.
It inspects metadata, summarizes collections, finds exact duplicates,
renames and organizes photos, compresses copies, makes contact sheets, interactively
reviews JPEGs, and removes EXIF GPS tags.
Normal output goes to standard output; diagnostics go to standard error.
.PP
macOS and Linux are the initial supported platforms.
Metadata inspection, statistics, timeline summaries, renaming, and sorting
recognize JPEG
(.jpg/.jpeg) and Sony ARW (.arw), ignoring extension case.
ARW support reads common TIFF/EXIF metadata only, without developing or
validating RAW pixels. Other RAW formats are unsupported.
Query, check, compress, contact, focus, review, and scrub remain JPEG-only; directory scans
skip ARW for those commands and single-file ARW input fails.
The
.B duplicates
command compares regular files of any type.
The
.B focus
command compares JPEG sharpness scores as a review aid.
.PP
Directory operations scan one directory level by default.
.B \-\-recursive
includes nested directories where supported.
Symlinks are not followed during scans.
Quote paths containing spaces.
Use
.B \-\-
to end option parsing before a path beginning with a dash.
Option values must be separate arguments; combined short flags and
.B \-\-option=value
syntax are not supported.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS review
.B photoc review
.I directory
.RB [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ "\-\-sort name|date" ]
.RI [ "\-\-show all|unmarked|picked|rejected" ]
.RI [ "\-\-images auto|iterm|none" ]
.RI [ "\-\-state file" ]
.PP
Interactively mark regular .jpg/.jpeg files as picked, rejected, or unmarked.
Exactly one directory is required; a single file and
.B \-\-json
are rejected. Both standard input and standard output must be terminals.
The default scan is flat; recursive scans do not follow symlinks. The discovered
list stays fixed for the session. JPEGs are never edited, moved, or deleted.
.TP
.B \-\-sort name|date
Name (default) sorts by case-sensitive bytewise basename, then relative path.
Date sorts valid EXIF capture times oldest first, with relative path ties;
missing or invalid dates come last. No filesystem-date fallback is used.
.TP
.B \-\-show all|unmarked|picked|rejected
Filter navigation, default all. Counts always cover the full discovered
collection. A mark that removes the current photo from the filter selects the
next match, or the previous match when it was last. An empty filter remains
usable for quitting.
.TP
.B \-\-images auto|iterm|none
Auto (default) uses iTerm2 inline images only in a directly detected iTerm2
session and otherwise shows text only. It excludes tmux and screen. iTerm
forces the image backend; none always uses text only. The original JPEG is
streamed to the terminal without writing another image. iTerm2 controls
display orientation. All modes show filename, status, metadata, sharpness,
counts, and controls.
.TP
.B \-\-state file
Use a different review-state JSON file; its parent must already exist. The
default is
.IR directory /.photoc-review.json.
The state stores schema version 1, the canonical absolute root, and picked
or rejected relative paths. Non-UTF-8 path bytes use hex fields. The first
actual mark change creates the file. Restarting restores marks and starts at
the first photo in the requested order/filter; cursor position is not saved.
Entries absent from the current discovery remain in the state file.
Malformed, unrelated, wrong-root, and unsupported-version state files are
refused. Updates use a checked, synced same-directory temporary file and
atomic rename. A failed save leaves the previous state and displayed mark
unchanged and returns status 1.
.PP
Right arrow, l, or Space moves forward; left arrow or h moves backward.
p picks, x rejects, u clears a mark, i toggles metadata detail, ? toggles
help, and q quits. Ctrl+C interrupts cleanly. Each changed mark is saved
before advancing. Sharpness is computed on first display and cached for the
session; it never marks automatically. Files that disappear or become
unreadable show as unavailable while navigation continues. Filename and
metadata control bytes are escaped before display. Terminal settings and
the alternate screen are restored on controlled exits and handled signals.
Quiet suppresses the post-exit informational summary, while the interactive
UI and errors remain visible. Verbose diagnostics stay out of the active UI.
No progress spinner is shown. See
.IR docs/review.md .
.SS query
.B photoc query
.I file|directory
.RI [ filters ]
.RB [ \-\-recursive ]
.RB [ \-\-json " | " \-\-print0 ]
.PP
Search JPEG metadata without modifying photographs.
All supplied filters are ANDed; missing or invalid queried fields do not match.
With no filters, every successfully parsed JPEG matches, even without EXIF.
Directory scans are flat by default and never follow symlinks.
Recursion requires a directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-camera " value"
Exact, case-sensitive EXIF camera model.
.TP
.BI \-\-make " value"
Exact, case-sensitive EXIF camera manufacturer.
.TP
.BI \-\-iso " expression"
Numeric ISO comparison: 100, =100, >100, >=100, <100, or <=100.
.TP
.BI \-\-aperture " expression"
Compare aperture f-number with the same operators.
.TP
.BI \-\-focal " expression"
Compare focal length in millimeters, not a 35 mm equivalent.
.TP
.BI \-\-after " YYYY-MM-DD"
Capture date on or after this day, including midnight.
.TP
.BI \-\-before " YYYY-MM-DD"
Capture date on or before this day, including 23:59:59.
.TP
.B \-\-has\-gps
Require valid latitude/longitude coordinates.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-gps
Require no valid coordinates, including absent or invalid GPS tags.
Exclusive with \-\-has\-gps.
.TP
.B \-\-print0
Emit NUL-separated paths, including a final NUL, for xargs \-0.
Exclusive with \-\-json.
.PP
Default stdout contains raw matching paths only, separated by newlines.
Paths are sorted bytewise and retain the supplied input directory prefix so
relative paths can be used by another command in the same working directory.
Use \-\-print0 for paths containing spaces or newlines; line output does not
escape filenames. A single-file path is preserved as supplied.
Quiet preserves results; verbose adds scan/match counts and mode on stderr.
.PP
Numbers are finite and nonnegative; decimals and scientific notation are
accepted. Whitespace, malformed expressions, NaN/infinity, and range errors
are usage errors. Equality compares loaded doubles directly.
Quote comparisons to protect shell redirection characters.
Dates must be real Gregorian dates (years 0001 through 9999); bounds are
inclusive and use DateTimeOriginal without timezone conversion or fallback
to filesystem timestamps. Inverted ranges are invalid.
Camera/make filters do not expand wildcards or match substrings.
Each valued filter may be supplied once; empty string filters are invalid.
.PP
JSON contains query (path, recursive, filters, string_matching, date_bounds),
matches (path and metadata), and summary (files_visited, jpeg_files_found,
photos_parsed, skipped_files, errors, matched).
Metadata includes camera, make, iso, aperture, focal_length_mm,
capture_timestamp, has_gps, latitude, longitude, width, and height.
Unavailable values are null; GPS filter is present, absent, or null.
JSON stdout remains JSON only at every verbosity level.
.PP
No matches returns 0. Individual JPEG load failures are reported on stderr,
including in quiet mode, and return 1 with successful directory matches.
Input, traversal, allocation, and single-file load failures leave stdout empty.
Invalid usage or conflicting options return 2.
The shared scanner buffers at most 32 regular entries and uses at most two
workers. Only matching metadata is retained before sorting.
Query does not fully decode images; use check for integrity auditing.
Invalid EXIF may become unavailable through the existing metadata loader.
.PP
Examples (jq is optional):
.nf
photoc query ./photos \-\-iso ">800" \-\-aperture "<=4"
photoc query ./photos \-\-recursive \-\-has\-gps \-\-print0 |
  xargs \-0 sh \-c 'for path do photoc exif \-\- "$path" || exit; done' sh
photoc query ./photos \-\-recursive \-\-json |
  jq \-r '.matches[] | [.path, .metadata.iso] | @tsv'
.fi
.PP
Use pipefail where supported to detect query failures in pipelines.
.SS check
.B photoc check
.I file|directory
.RB [ \-\-recursive ]
.RB [ \-\-only\-errors ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Audit JPEG marker structure, decode every pixel scanline, and inspect standard
EXIF directory/value bounds and libexif corruption diagnostics.
Read-only: no repair, recompression, renaming, deletion, or sidecar writing.
Directory scans include regular .jpg/.jpeg files, case-insensitively.
Symlinks are never followed; recursion requires a directory.
Rows are sorted by path, relative to a directory input.
OK means structure and pixels are readable without a detected issue.
WARNING means pixels decode with malformed/unreadable EXIF, unusual structure,
or a recoverable decoder condition.
ERROR means decoding failed or an operational/resource/unsupported condition
prevented a reliable complete audit.
Some truncated JPEGs are recovered by the decoder and remain WARNING; the
photograph may still be incomplete.
Warnings return status 0; one or more errors return status 1.
.B \-\-only\-errors
excludes OK and WARNING rows, in human and JSON output.
Summary counts always cover all checked files.
Quiet retains rows and counts but suppresses the explanatory footer.
Errors remain on stderr; verbose adds mode, paths, recursion, serial worker
count, and checked/skipped/warning/error counts on stderr.
JSON contains summary fields files_checked, ok, warnings, errors, files_skipped,
and a files array with path, status, code, and message per row.
Codes are stable identifiers; one primary finding is reported per file.
All JSON stdout remains JSON only.
Usage, unsupported input paths, and traversal failures leave stdout empty.
Directory checks continue after per-file errors; empty directories succeed.
.PP
Compressed input is streamed into a bounded buffer, and decoded output uses
one row. Multi-scan coefficient storage is bounded; files are checked serially.
Limits are 512 MiB per file, 100 million pixels, 256 scans, and estimated
multi-scan coefficient storage of 256 MiB.
EXIF audits bound directory graphs to 64 directories and aggregate value/entry
storage to 4 MiB.
Resource-limit and unsupported-process errors mean not fully checked, not
proven corruption.
This is not a visual-quality, authenticity, backup, or comprehensive metadata
validator. ICC/XMP contents, MakerNote internals, thumbnail contents, and data
after the first image's EOI are outside the audit.
The OS may update access times; ordinary size/timestamp changes during reading
are detected, but the audit is not an atomic filesystem snapshot.
.SS compress
.B photoc compress
.I file|directory
.RI [ "\-\-quality 1-100" ]
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ "\-\-output-dir directory" ]
.br
.B photoc compress
.I file|directory
.BI "\-\-target " size
.RI [ "\-\-min-quality 1-100" ]
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ "\-\-output-dir directory" ]
.PP
Re-encode JPEGs into new copies using lossy compression.
Quality defaults to 80.
.B \-\-target
searches for the highest quality fitting the requested size, with a default
minimum quality of 20.
.B \-\-quality
and
.B \-\-target
cannot be combined;
.B \-\-min-quality
requires
.BR \-\-target .
Sizes are positive integers in bytes, optionally suffixed by
.BR B ,
.BR KB ,
.BR MB ,
.BR GB ,
.BR KiB ,
.BR MiB ,
or
.BR GiB .
Suffixes are case-sensitive.
Decimal units use powers of 1000; binary units use powers of 1024.
.PP
Output names insert
.B .compressed
before the original extension, preserving extension case.
Without
.BR \-\-output-dir ,
copies are placed beside sources.
An output directory preserves paths relative to the input directory.
.B \-\-recursive
requires directory input.
Existing outputs fail single-file operations and are skipped in directory mode.
Directory scans skip existing
.B .compressed
copies.
.PP
Report original/output sizes, chosen quality, and savings.
Re-encoding can increase file size.
An unreachable target writes a best-effort copy at the minimum quality and
returns status 1.
EXIF APP1 payloads (including Orientation and GPS), ICC_PROFILE APP2 chunks,
and standard/Extended Adobe XMP APP1 packets are preserved byte for byte
in their original relative order, in quality and target-size modes.
EXIF passes the existing validity checks and is no longer rebuilt for compression.
ICC chunk counts and sequence indices must be complete, unique, and consistent,
with nonempty total profile data; invalid sets fail before publishing a copy.
No ICC interpretation or conversion is performed.
Grayscale images with ICC remain grayscale; CMYK/YCCK is unsupported by the
existing RGB decoder.
Standard and Extended XMP packets are preserved as opaque bytes; XML, GUID
links, and extended completeness are not validated or repaired.
Recognized metadata after the first scan is refused.
Metadata snapshots are capped at 64 MiB including bookkeeping; excessive
metadata fails the file with status 1.
Other original APP markers, comments, Photoshop/IPTC resources, and MPF/MPO
image-offset data are not copied.
JPEG coding tables and scan data are generated afresh.
Raw dimensions and EXIF Orientation are retained without rotating pixels.
Preserved metadata contributes to target-size calculations.
The temporary output is decoded and its dimensions/metadata verified before
publication without overwriting originals.
Compression is not a privacy scrub; XMP location data is also retained.
.SS exif
.B photoc exif
.I file
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Inspect one JPEG or Sony ARW without modifying it.
Report filename/path, format, file size, dimensions, orientation, camera make/model, ISO, aperture,
exposure time, focal length, capture timestamp, and available GPS coordinates.
Missing fields are unavailable in terminal output and null in JSON.
JSON adds file.format (jpeg or sony_arw) and image.orientation (1-8 or null).
ARW dimensions come from EXIF pixel dimensions, or the largest non-reduced
CFA/linear-RAW directory; preview dimensions are not substituted.
Stored dimensions are not rotated and may include sensor margins.
Exposure time uses seconds; focal length uses millimeters.
Capture time is the recorded EXIF DateTimeOriginal string, without timezone
conversion or a filesystem-date fallback.
.PP
JSON contains
.BR file ,
.BR image ,
.BR camera ,
.BR exposure ,
.BR date ,
and
.B location
objects.
The
.B has_gps
field is true only when both coordinates can be decoded as valid values.
False does not prove that all location tags are absent.
.SS duplicates
.B photoc duplicates
.I directory
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Report exact duplicate groups for regular files of any type.
Filter candidates by size and leading content, then compare SHA-256 digests.
No files are deleted or changed.
Visually similar images, or images with different metadata or encoding, are
not exact duplicates.
.PP
Report duplicate-group count, all files in those groups, and potential savings
assuming one retained copy per group.
Groups and paths have deterministic ordering.
Hard-linked paths can be counted separately; savings describe logical file
bytes, not guaranteed freed disk space.
File read failures are warned about and skipped; readable groups can still be
reported with exit status 1.
.PP
JSON contains
.BR directory ,
.BR recursive ,
.BR duplicate_group_count ,
.BR duplicate_file_count ,
.BR potential_savings_bytes ,
and
.BR groups .
Each group contains
.BR file_size_bytes ,
.BR sha256 ,
and
.BR paths .
.SS stats
.B photoc stats
.I directory
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Summarize parsed JPEG/ARW photos, total/average file size, earliest/latest valid capture
dates, and camera-model, ISO, aperture, and focal-length distributions.
Missing fields are omitted from distributions.
Sort counts by descending frequency, then camera-model lexical order or
ascending numeric value for ties.
Most-used values are the first entries in those sorted distributions.
Percentages use all parsed photos as the denominator, so a distribution can
sum to less than 100 percent.
.PP
Photos with missing optional fields still contribute photo counts and file sizes.
Individual broken or unreadable JPEG/ARW metadata is warned about and counted as errors.
These per-file warnings alone do not change a successful exit status.
Inspect the scan summary for completeness.
.PP
JSON contains
.BR scan ,
.BR storage ,
.BR capture_dates ,
and
.B distributions
objects.
The photo count is
.BR scan.photos_parsed ;
the file-error count is
.BR scan.errors .
.B scan.jpeg_files_found
counts JPEG candidates,
.B scan.arw_files_found
counts ARW candidates, and
.B scan.metadata_files_found
counts both, including load failures.
Empty distributions are arrays; unavailable dates and average/median size are null.
.PP
Additional statistics append to the original human report. JSON adds standard
EXIF LensModel usage, explicit 35mm-equivalent focal lengths, shutter seconds,
landscape/portrait/square orientation, exact stored resolutions with megapixels,
and photos by YYYY, YYYY-MM, YYYY-MM-DD, and local hour 0-23.
All percentages retain parsed photos as the denominator; unavailable contains
missing/invalid counts for every distribution and file size.
Orientation values 5-8 swap dimensions for classification; resolution buckets
retain stored dimensions. Megapixels are width times height divided by 1,000,000.
Standard EXIF FocalLengthIn35mmFilm is reported separately from physical focal
length. No MakerNotes, lens inference, or crop-factor guesses are used.
.PP
storage.median_file_size_bytes is the middle known size, or the arithmetic mean
of the middle pair for even counts. With no known sizes it is null.
sessions sorts valid capture times and applies the existing default 60-minute
gap rule: exactly 60 minutes joins, greater than 60 minutes starts a new session.
Missing/invalid dates are excluded, and sessions can cross calendar boundaries.
The summary reports gap_minutes, dated_photos, unavailable_photos, count,
average_photos_per_session, smallest_session, and largest_session.
Average photos per session divides dated photos by sessions. With none, the
count is zero and the average/minimum/maximum are null. Times are recorded local
EXIF times without timezone conversion. Only scalar metadata samples are retained.
No stats --by option is introduced; JSON views can be selected with jq.
See docs/stats.md for definitions and the additive JSON schema.
.SS timeline
.B photoc timeline
.I directory
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-gap " duration" ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Read-only chronological shooting overview from JPEG/ARW metadata.
Use valid DateTimeOriginal values in YYYY:MM:DD HH:MM:SS form, sort by capture
time, and group first by recorded calendar date, then by session.
Missing and invalid timestamps are excluded and counted separately;
there is no filesystem-date fallback or guessed assignment.
.PP
The default gap is 60 minutes, shared with sort --by session.
Within each date, a gap greater than the threshold starts a session;
an exact-threshold gap joins. Compare consecutive photos, so total session
duration can exceed the gap. Duplicate timestamps join even with a zero gap.
Midnight always starts another date and session, unlike sort/stats sessions,
which can cross calendar boundaries. Missing timestamps do not split the
remaining dated-photo chain.
.PP
Gap values are nonnegative integer minutes (30m) or hours (2h), with lowercase
suffixes, up to 4,294,967,295 minutes. Fractions, seconds, negative values,
uppercase suffixes, and bare numbers are invalid.
.PP
Each session reports start/end clock times, duration in seconds, photo count,
total known logical file size in bytes, most-used physical focal length in mm,
most-used aperture, ISO minimum/maximum, and camera models when available.
Numeric frequency ties select the smaller value. Camera models use exact
strings, descending counts, then case-sensitive lexical ties.
Missing fields are excluded; no 35mm equivalent or camera crop factor is
guessed.
A single-photo session has zero duration.
Counts are per path, not unique content.
.PP
Times are recorded EXIF clock values with no timezone conversion,
daylight-saving correction, or clock synchronization.
No timezone is inferred from the computer
or GPS. Duration is the recorded clock difference, not verified elapsed time.
Photos from cameras with different clocks can group or order unexpectedly.
.PP
JSON contains directory, recursive, gap_minutes, time_basis
(recorded_local_exif), summary, and dates. Each date object contains date
(YYYY-MM-DD) and a chronological sessions array. Sessions contain start/end
(HH:MM:SS without offset), duration_seconds, photo_count,
total_file_size_bytes, photos_with_file_size, most_used_focal_length_mm,
most_used_aperture, iso_min, iso_max, and camera_models.
Optional numeric values are null; camera_models contains model/photo_count
objects and is empty when unavailable. Numeric fields remain numbers.
.PP
Summary counters are files_visited, photos_parsed, photos_included,
photos_skipped_timestamp, missing_timestamp, invalid_timestamp,
metadata_errors, unsupported_files, dates, and sessions.
Missing plus invalid equals photos_skipped_timestamp; included plus skipped
equals photos_parsed. Symlinks and non-regular entries are excluded.
Quiet mode retains sessions and all result counts while hiding the introductory
status text and non-critical warnings. Verbose diagnostics go to standard error;
JSON fields do not change with verbosity.
.PP
Per-file metadata failures are warned about, skipped, and counted; they alone
return success, as with stats. Empty directories and unavailable timestamps
also succeed. Fatal scan, allocation, aggregation, or output errors return 1;
invalid usage returns 2. This is a metadata overview, not a pixel integrity
audit. See docs/timeline.md for the JSON contract and definitions.
.SS rename
.B photoc rename
.I directory
.BI "\-\-format " template
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-apply ]
.PP
Preview JPEG/ARW renames using a filename template.
Files remain in their original directories.
Only
.B \-\-apply
authorizes changes after the entire plan passes preflight.
There is no separate dry-run flag.
.PP
Placeholders are
.BR {date} ,
.BR {datetime} ,
.BR {camera} ,
.BR {make} ,
.BR {iso} ,
.BR {aperture} ,
.BR {focal} ,
.BR {sequence} ,
.BR {original} ,
and
.BR {ext} .
Date and datetime use
.B YYYY-MM-DD
and
.BR YYYY-MM-DD_HH-MM-SS .
Camera is the model; original is the basename without its final extension.
Extension omits the dot and preserves source casing.
Sequence starts at 0001 in lexical source-path order across the scan.
Unsafe filename characters become underscores.
Use doubled braces for literal braces.
.PP
Missing required metadata, invalid JPEG/ARW metadata, existing destinations, and duplicate
proposed destinations block entries.
Any blocked entry prevents all changes with
.BR \-\-apply .
Dry runs still show safe rows and return status 1 when entries are blocked.
Report source-to-destination rows and planned/unchanged/blocked/applied/
rolled-back counts.
.SS sort
.B photoc sort
.I directory
.B \-\-by date|session
.RI [ "\-\-gap duration" ]
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-apply ]
.PP
Preview JPEG/ARW moves into directories under the input root while preserving filenames.
Date mode uses
.BR YYYY/MM/DD/ .
Session mode uses
.BR session-001/ ,
.BR session-002/ ,
and so on, ordered by capture time with source-path ties.
.PP
A session continues when the gap from the previous photo does not exceed the
threshold.
The default is 60 minutes.
.B \-\-gap
accepts nonnegative integer minutes or hours, such as
.B 30m
or
.BR 2h ,
and is valid only with session mode.
An exact-threshold gap remains in the same session.
IDs can change when the scanned set or gap changes.
.PP
Missing/invalid capture dates are blocked, without filesystem-date fallback.
Existing destinations, duplicate planned destinations, and unsafe parent
directories also block moves.
Any blocked entry prevents all moves with
.BR \-\-apply .
Dry run creates no directories.
Apply creates needed directories and uses exclusive moves; no copy-and-delete
fallback is provided across filesystems.
Report plan rows and summary counts as for
.BR rename .
.SS contact
.B photoc contact
.I directory
.BI "\-\-output " sheet.jpg
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ "\-\-columns 1-8" ]
.RI [ "\-\-thumb-size 96-512" ]
.RI [ "\-\-quality 1-100" ]
.RI [ \-\-metadata ]
.RI [ "\-\-sort name|date" ]
.PP
Generate JPEG contact sheets with an aspect-preserving thumbnail and filename
per tile. Optional exposure lines show available aperture, shutter, ISO, and
focal length. Defaults are four columns, 240-pixel thumbnail boxes, JPEG
quality 85, and filename sorting. Date sorting uses EXIF capture time;
undated photos follow dated photos. Recursion includes nested directories.
.PP
One page uses the requested output path. Multiple pages insert -001, -002,
and so on before its extension. Each page contains at most 24 photos and six
rows. All output paths are checked first; existing files are never overwritten.
Each page is validated and published through a temporary file. Earlier pages
remain if a later page fails.
.PP
EXIF orientation is applied to rendered thumbnails. Source metadata and ICC
profiles are not copied into sheets; no color-profile conversion is performed.
Non-sRGB source colors may differ. Labels use an internal bitmap font; long
names are shortened, and non-ASCII characters display as question marks.
.PP
At most 1,000 JPEGs are scanned, each source file may be at most 64 MiB, and
each RGB canvas may be at most 64 MiB and 8,192 pixels per side. Oversized
layouts fail without an unbounded allocation. Bad JPEGs and metadata loads
return status 1; invalid options return status 2. See docs/contact.md for
details.
.SS focus
.B photoc focus
.I file|directory
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-threshold " value" ]
.RI [ \-\-only\-blurry ]
.RI [ \-\-json ]
.PP
Report JPEG sharpness scores, sorted from lowest to highest with path ordering
for ties. Scores are variance of a Laplacian on grayscale pixels, using scaled
JPEG decoding with a maximum analysis dimension of 1024.
Scores strictly below
.B \-\-threshold
(default 100; finite and nonnegative) are labeled "possibly blurry".
.B \-\-only\-blurry
filters rows; summary counts and minimum/average/maximum cover all analyzed JPEGs.
The threshold is a review hint, not proof of blur or artistic quality.
Noise, texture, subject matter, and JPEG artifacts affect the score.
Missing EXIF is valid; directory scans skip non-JPEG files and continue past
unreadable or invalid JPEGs, returning status 1 if any JPEG fails.
JSON contains the input path, threshold, recursion and filtering settings,
a photos array with path, numeric score, threshold, and possibly_blurry boolean,
and summary counts and minimum/average/maximum scores.
Photo paths are relative to the scanned directory, or as supplied for a single
file. Summary scores are null when no JPEG could be analyzed.
Filtering does not change the summary; diagnostics remain on standard error.
.SS scrub
.B photoc scrub
.I file|directory
.B \-\-gps
.RI [ \-\-recursive ]
.RI [ \-\-in-place ]
.PP
Remove EXIF GPS directory entries and the GPS pointer.
By default, write a new copy with
.B .scrubbed
inserted before the original extension.
An existing copy destination causes failure without overwriting it.
Files without EXIF GPS are skipped without creating a copy.
Directory scans skip existing
.B .scrubbed
copies.
.B \-\-recursive
requires directory input.
.PP
JPEG image bytes are preserved without recompression.
Ordinary non-GPS EXIF is retained where possible; unusual MakerNotes may change.
Location data in XMP, MakerNotes, filenames, or visible content is outside the
removal scope.
Report processed, skipped, failed, and successfully GPS-removed file counts.
.PP
.B \-\-in-place
replaces each original only after writing, syncing, and verifying a temporary
JPEG beside it.
It creates no backup.
Symlinks, hard-linked files, differently owned files, and changed sources are
refused.
POSIX permission bits and group ownership are preserved; ACLs and extended
attributes are not copied.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options may appear before or after the command.
Long-running directory commands show delayed, throttled progress on standard
error when it is a terminal. Progress does not change JSON or other result
data on standard output. Redirected standard error, quiet mode, and
.B \-\-no-progress
disable progress.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print global help, or command help when a command is supplied.
Exit successfully without executing the command.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
Cannot be combined with a command or
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Keep normal output and add diagnostics on standard error: operating mode,
recursion, file discovery/skip/failure counts, and relevant input/output paths.
Statistics, timeline, and duplicate scans report the worker limit;
Small workloads and worker startup failures can run serially.
Cannot be combined with quiet (status 2), regardless of option order.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Keep requested result data: EXIF fields, statistics/distributions, duplicate
groups/counts/savings, focus scores, query paths, check rows/counts,
timeline sessions and skipped/error counts, and rename/sort mappings.
Suppress scan status, operation summaries, and non-critical warnings.
Successful compress and scrub operations produce no standard output.
Errors explaining non-zero exits remain on standard error, including warnings
for failed duplicate/focus processing and blocked rename/sort entries.
Help and version output remain available.
Cannot be combined with verbose (status 2), regardless of option order.
.TP
.B \-\-no-progress
Disable interactive progress display regardless of terminal detection.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print structured JSON for
.BR exif ,
.BR stats ,
.BR timeline ,
.BR duplicates ,
.BR focus ,
.BR check ,
and
.BR query .
Optional metadata is null, presence fields are booleans, and numbers remain
numeric.
No human-readable decorations are printed on standard output.
Verbosity does not change the JSON schema or its scan/error counters.
Verbose diagnostics, warnings, and errors use standard error; quiet suppresses
non-critical warnings. No JSON error envelope is emitted.
Other implemented commands reject this option with status 2.
.SH SAFETY
.B review
changes only its separate JSON state file; it never modifies JPEG bytes,
metadata, filenames, or locations. It does not delete picked or rejected
photos. A state-save failure keeps the last persisted marks.
.PP
.B rename
and
.B sort
preview by default.
Apply checks the whole plan before changing files and never overwrites an
existing destination.
An apply failure triggers rollback attempts, but concurrent filesystem changes
or interruption can prevent full restoration.
They are not crash-safe transactions.
.PP
.B compress
and default
.B scrub
write new files immediately and preserve originals.
Temporary JPEGs are verified before finalizing without overwriting destinations.
Directory operations can retain earlier successful outputs after later failures.
.B scrub \-\-in-place
is explicit replacement with no backup or directory-wide rollback.
Keep backups and avoid concurrent changes when modifying important collections.
.SH EXAMPLES
Cull a JPEG directory, then resume with unmarked photos:
.PP
.nf
photoc review ./shoot \-\-images none
photoc review ./shoot \-\-sort date \-\-show unmarked
.fi
.PP
Inspect JPEG/ARW metadata and save a machine-readable report:
.PP
.nf
photoc exif photo.jpg
photoc exif DSC00001.ARW
photoc exif photo.jpg \-\-json > metadata.json
.fi
.PP
Summarize a collection and report exact duplicates:
.PP
.nf
photoc stats ./photos \-\-recursive
photoc timeline ./photos \-\-recursive \-\-gap 30m
photoc duplicates ./photos \-\-recursive \-\-json
.fi
.PP
Write compressed copies:
.PP
.nf
photoc compress photo.jpg \-\-quality 75
photoc compress photo.jpg \-\-target 2MB \-\-min-quality 30
photoc compress ./photos \-\-recursive \-\-output-dir ./compressed
.fi
.PP
Preview renames, then apply the reviewed naming scheme:
.PP
.nf
photoc rename ./photos \-\-format "{date}_{camera}_{sequence}.{ext}"
photoc rename ./photos \-\-format "{date}_{camera}_{sequence}.{ext}" \-\-apply
.fi
.PP
Preview date or session folders:
.PP
.nf
photoc sort ./photos \-\-by date \-\-recursive
photoc sort ./photos \-\-by session \-\-gap 30m
.fi
.PP
Remove EXIF GPS tags from a copy; explicit replacement requires a backup:
.PP
.nf
photoc scrub photo.jpg \-\-gps
photoc scrub photo.jpg \-\-gps \-\-in-place
.fi
.PP
Review possible low-sharpness photos:
.PP
.nf
photoc focus ./photos \-\-recursive \-\-threshold 100 \-\-only\-blurry
photoc focus ./photos \-\-recursive \-\-json > focus.json
.fi
.PP
Create contact sheets for reviewing a shoot:
.PP
.nf
photoc contact ./photos \-\-output sheet.jpg \-\-metadata
.fi
.PP
Inspect a path beginning with a dash:
.PP
.nf
photoc exif \-\- \-photo.jpg
.fi
.SH FILES
.B photoc
does not currently read a configuration file or maintain a photo database.
.TP
.I .photoc-review.json
Default review-state file under the canonical review root. Stores version,
root identity, and only picked/rejected relative paths. Override with
.BR review " " \-\-state .
.TP
.I *.compressed.jpg, *.compressed.jpeg
New compression outputs, with original extension casing retained.
.TP
.I *.scrubbed.jpg, *.scrubbed.jpeg
GPS-scrubbed copies beside originals, with extension casing retained.
.TP
.I YYYY/MM/DD/
Date-sort destination directories beneath the supplied scan root.
.TP
.I session-NNN/
Session-sort destination directories beneath the supplied scan root.
.TP
Temporary JPEGs
Created beside destinations for compression and metadata writing;
in-place scrub uses the source directory.
.TP
.I docs/
Command guides and JSON schemas in the source repository.
.TP
.I prefix/share/man/man1/photoc.1
Default installed manual location.
CMake's install prefix and install-directory settings can change this path.
.SH EXIT STATUSES
.TP
.B 0
Success, including empty collections, unchanged plans, missing optional EXIF,
and allowed skips.
.B stats
and
.B timeline
can return success with per-file warnings recorded in their summaries.
.TP
.B 1
Operational failure, including unsupported input formats, invalid JPEG/ARW metadata,
I/O or output errors, blocked rename/sort plans, failed modifications, or
compression targets not met.
Partial reports or successful copies/modifications may already exist.
.B duplicates
returns this status after individual file-read warnings.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage: unknown command/option, missing arguments or values,
invalid option values, or incompatible options.
.TP
.B 3
Reserved for commands that are not implemented.
.SH BUGS
ARW support is limited to standard classic-TIFF/EXIF/GPS tags: dimensions,
make/model, DateTimeOriginal, ISO Speed Ratings, FNumber, ExposureTime,
FocalLength, latitude/longitude, and orientation.
Both TIFF byte orders are supported. Missing values remain unavailable.
BigTIFF, DNG, other RAW extensions, and explicit non-Sony makes are unsupported.
With Make absent, .arw selects the backend without verifying the manufacturer.
Sony MakerNotes, encrypted metadata, XMP sidecars, ICC, and sensor data are not
interpreted; readable metadata does not prove RAW image integrity.
.PP
The bounded reader rejects malformed offsets/lengths, repeated directory
pointers, duplicate/unsorted tags, and cycles. Limits are UINT32_MAX bytes per
file, 64 directories, 16,384 entries, 2 MiB metadata reads, 64 KiB per selected
value, and 256 KiB retained values. Resource failures do not prove corruption.
Automated fixtures are generated metadata containers, not camera originals;
compatibility with every Sony camera generation is not established.
See docs/raw.md for the support matrix, bounds, and optional real-camera test.
.PP
Metadata summaries are not complete EXIF dumps, and metadata rewriting does
not guarantee preservation of every proprietary tag.
EXIF GPS removal is not removal of every possible location trace.
Capture timestamps are treated as recorded local times without timezone handling.
.PP
Report reproducible bugs at:
.UR https://github.com/ahmetomerv/photoc/issues
photoc issue tracker
.UE
.PP
Include the
.B photoc \-\-version
output, operating system, exact invocation, exit status, and relevant diagnostics.
Use a small synthetic or privacy-reviewed fixture when needed; avoid publishing
private photos or GPS information.
